# __PLUGIN_NAME__

A plugin for the [Auth0 CLI](https://github.com/auth0/auth0-cli), written in TypeScript with the [auth0](https://www.npmjs.com/package/auth0) Node package and compiled to one executable with [Bun](https://bun.sh). It runs as `auth0 __PLUGIN_NAME__` and calls the Management API through the CLI, which never gives the plugin a real token.

The sample command lists the clients of your tenant. Make it your own.

## Requirements

- Bun 1.4 or later
- The Auth0 CLI, with plugin development turned on: `export AUTH0_CLI_PLUGIN_DEV_MODE=true`

## Layout

`src/index.ts` is the entry point and `src/run.ts` finds the command and turns errors into exit codes. `src/commands/` holds one file per command and the list of them. `src/lib/` holds the shared code: environment, SDK client, output, error text with exit codes, flags and pagination. `plugin.json` is the manifest: commands, flags and the Management API scopes each command may use.

## Build and run

```sh
bun install          # also writes bun.lock; commit it
bun run build        # writes ./__PLUGIN_NAME__ for this machine
```

Link the build to the CLI once. The link reads `plugin.json` and the executable again on every run, so after a change you only rebuild.

```sh
auth0 plugin dev link
auth0 plugin approve __PLUGIN_NAME__    # a person reviews the scopes; repeat when they change
auth0 __PLUGIN_NAME__
auth0 __PLUGIN_NAME__ --json
```

`bun test` runs the tests, `bun run typecheck` checks the types, and `auth0 plugin dev validate` checks `plugin.json`.

## Scopes

The CLI proxy refuses every call that needs a scope the command did not declare, and the plugin then says which scope is missing. Declare scopes under `permissions.scopes` of the command in `plugin.json`, then approve again. The sample command needs `read:clients`.

