## Add a command

1. Copy `src/commands/clients.ts` to `src/commands/<name>.ts`. Change the name, the help text and the code in `run`.
2. Import it in `src/commands/index.ts` and add it to the list.
3. Add the command to `commands` in `plugin.json`, with its description and the scopes it needs. Rebuild. A command that is not the root command runs as `auth0 __PLUGIN_NAME__ <name>`.

Flags: declare them with `parseFlags` in the command and list them under `flags` in `plugin.json`; the flags of the CLI itself (`--json`, `--tenant` and so on) are handled for you. Use `out.json` to choose between `out.printJson` and `out.printTable`, `collect` for every page of a list call, and pass `signal` to every SDK call so that Ctrl-C and the CLI's timeout stop it. Results go to stdout, anything else to stderr.

## Environment

The CLI sets these for every run. Only `src/lib/env.ts` reads them.

| Variable | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `AUTH0_PLUGIN_DOMAIN` | Address of the CLI proxy (`host:port`) |
| `AUTH0_PLUGIN_MANAGEMENT_TOKEN` | A secret for this run that lets the plugin use the proxy |
| `AUTH0_PLUGIN_JSON`, `AUTH0_PLUGIN_JSON_COMPACT` | `true` when the user asked for JSON output (agent mode turns it on) |
| `AUTH0_PLUGIN_TENANT` | The tenant the run is for |
| `NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS` | The certificate of the proxy, which Bun trusts without code |

Exit codes: 0 on success, 1 when the command fails, 2 when the arguments are wrong.

## Releasing

Edit nothing by hand except `version` in `plugin.json` (a `version` in `package.json` is kept in step). Run one command, then merge the pull request it opens.

```sh
bun run release patch              # or minor, or major
bun run release 1.2.3              # an exact version
bun run release patch --dry-run    # show the steps, change nothing
```

It needs `git`, `gh` (`gh auth login`) and a clean `main` that matches `origin`. It creates `release/v<version>`, raises the version, commits `chore: release v<version>` with your own git settings, pushes and opens the pull request. A keyword refuses a prerelease version; give the exact version then.

Merging runs the Release workflow, which:

1. runs the tests (`ci.yml`) on the merged commit,
2. compiles one executable per platform with `bun build --compile`,
3. packs each with `plugin.json` into `<name>_<version>_<platform>.tar.gz` (`.zip` on Windows),
4. creates the release `v<version>` at that commit with the archives, `checksums.txt` and generated notes.

If a run fails half way, re-run it or run the workflow by hand on `main`: it finishes what is missing and does nothing once the release is complete. The first push of a new project releases the scaffolded version. The workflow listens to `main`; change that in `release.yml` for another branch name.

| File | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| `scripts/release.ts` | The program behind `bun run release`; `scripts/version.ts` holds the version rules |
| `.github/workflows/ci.yml` | Install, typecheck, test and build; the release calls it too |
| `.github/workflows/release.yml` | The steps above |
| `.github/actions/read-version/` | Reads the version and tells whether its tag and release exist (`.github/scripts/`) |
| `.github/actions/build-archive/` | Compiles and packs one platform (`build-archive.sh`), uploads the archive |

Every platform of `plugin.json` is built with `bun build --compile --target=bun-<platform>`, for example `bun-linux-x64-musl`.

The workflow stops at the GitHub release. Signing, attestations and publishing to the plugin catalog come later.

